  <preface xml:id="preface-2026">
    <title>Preface to the 2026 Edition</title>


		<p>
			We are publishing this edition of <em>Calculus in Context</em> online to make it freely available to all users. It is essentially unchanged from the 1994 edition.
		</p>

		<p>
			The continuing support of Five Colleges, Inc., and especially of the Five College Coordinator, Lorna Peterson, has been crucial in paving the way for this new edition. We also wish to thank the many colleagues who have shared with us their experiences in using the book over the last twenty years—and have provided us with corrections to the text.
		</p>
		

<p>The 2008 edition of  <em>Calculus in Context</em> was published online to
make it freely available to all users.  With the 2026 distribution, we are now 
making the book and the source files available under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike license (CC BY-SA).  The text is essentially the same as the 2008 edition with minor corrections and updates and other changes noted below.</p>

<p><em>The Source Files</em> The release of the source files makes it possible for people to adapt the text to their own uses.  The Creative Commons license ensures that the original creators will get credit (BY) and the adaptations will continue to be available for others to use (SA).  See the copyright page for more details.</p>

<p>The source files consist of LaTeX files and encapsulated postscript (eps) files.  Also, five illustrations in chapter~12 (Case Studies) use ps files that have unfortunately been lost.  These illustrations were recreated for the 2026 distribution, along with improvements to other illustrations.</p>
 
<p><em>Supplemental Material</em> We want to mention some items described in the 1995 preface:</p>
 
<ol>>
<li> The Handbook for Instructors for Calculus in Context was written by Kenneth Hoffman and is described on page viii.  A pdf of the Handbook is included with the 2026 distribution, with one exception noted below.</li>

<li> As mentioned on page xi, the original Handbook contained solutions to all exercises in the book.  The solutions now appear in a separate pdf included with the 2026  distribution, along with the source files.</li>

<li> Page x mentions computer programs that were previously available at emmy.smith.edu via ftp.  These QBASIC programs, based on code used in the text, now appear in appendix A and have been updated to work in QB64 (freely available for Mac and Windows computers).  Source code can be found in the LaTeX file for appendix A. Translating these programs into a Mathematica notebook or a Maple package should be a straightforward process for interested instructors.</li>

<li>  In addition to appendix A, the 2026 distribution includes appendices B (a quick reference guide) and C (answers to selected exercises) from the 1995 edition.   The appendices were omitted in the 2008 edition.</li>
</ol>

<p><em>How to Use the 2026 Distribution.</em> We see three ways to use these files:</p>

<ol>
<li> <bf>Adapting.</bf> Over the years, several instructors have requested the source files of the book so they can adapt the material for their own purposes.  The 2026 distribution means that these files are now available to everyone, subject to the CC BY-SA license.  The solutions will help potential adapters judge the educational value and difficulty of the exercises in the text, and our approach to the material, as explained in the 1995 preface, should adapt nicely to active learning or IBL environments (or whatever new approaches arise in the future).</li>

<li> <bf>Self-Studying.</bf>  For someone who wants to learn the ideas of calculus on their own, the pdfs of the 2026 edition, the handbook, and the solutions could be invaluable tools for the reasons explained in the 1995 preface and the \textbf{To the Student} on pages xiii-xiv.  As always, we urge readers to do the exercises on their own and only consult the solutions to check their work or provide hints if they get stuck.  The goal is to take an active approach to the learning process.</li>

<li> <bf>Teaching.</bf> The 1995 edition, together with the handbook, were designed for classroom use, and the 2026 versions could still be used for this purpose, with some tweaks regarding modern technology.  One challenge is that for the reasons stated above, the solutions are part of the 2026 distribution, which means that students might have easy access to solutions.  Instructors will need to create a positive atmosphere in the classroom so that students will use the solutions in a way that does not interfere with their active learning of the material.</li>
</ol> 


  </preface>
